course: geography
lesson: 24
title: Population and cities
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Imagine everyone on Earth standing in one long queue: about 8.2 billion people today. In 1337, the year Europa Universalis 5 begins, the queue was perhaps only about 400 million long, fewer people than India alone has now.
explain: People are not spread evenly like butter on toast. They crowd into river valleys, coastal plains and fertile lowlands, and leave deserts, high mountains and icy lands nearly empty; look at Egypt, where almost everyone lives in the thin green strip along the Nile.
explain: Cities grew at useful spots: where a river could be bridged, where a harbour was sheltered, where trade routes met. London grew at the lowest point where the Thames could be bridged.
explain: For most of history nearly everyone lived on the land and a city of 100,000 was huge. Since about 2007 more than half of all people have lived in towns and cities, and some cities now hold over 10 million.
explain: The game's starting map hides a disaster: just ten years after 1337 the Black Death reached Europe and killed perhaps a third to a half of its people. Real numbers are only estimates, so historians give ranges, not exact counts like a game screen.
fact: About 8.2 billion people live on Earth in 2026, compared with perhaps about 400 million around 1337.
fact: India and China are the two most populous countries, each with about 1.4 billion people, and India overtook China around 2023.
fact: Most people live in fairly small parts of the land: river valleys, coastal plains and fertile lowlands, while deserts, high mountains and polar lands are nearly empty.
fact: Since about 2007 more than half the world's people have lived in towns and cities.
fact: A megacity has more than 10 million people, and there are now over 30 of them, most in Asia, including Tokyo, Delhi, Shanghai, Dhaka, São Paulo, Cairo and Lagos.
fact: The largest cities of the 1300s, such as Constantinople, Paris or Hangzhou, had a few hundred thousand people at most.
fact: The Black Death killed perhaps a third to a half of Europe's people between 1347 and 1351.
fact: Population density means how many people live on each square kilometre, and Bangladesh has over 1,000 while Australia has about 3.
fact: Some countries, such as Japan and Italy, have many old people and few births, while countries such as Niger have very young populations; with few births there will later be fewer young workers to look after the old.
fact: The median age splits the people in half, half younger and half older; in Niger it is under 16, and in Japan it is about 49.
fact: Most great cities grew at useful spots: river crossings, natural harbours, estuaries or meeting points of trade routes.
fact: Migration means people moving to live in another place, inside their country or abroad.
exercise: About how many people live on Earth in 2026? | About 8.2 billion | About 820 million | About 82 billion | About 2.8 billion
exercise: About how many people lived on Earth around 1337? | Perhaps about 400 million | About 8 billion, as today | About 4 million | About 40 billion
exercise: Which two countries have the most people? | India and China | Russia and Canada | The USA and Brazil | Australia and Japan
exercise: Around when did India overtake China in population? | Around 2023 | Around 1337 | Around 1900 | It never has
exercise: Where do most people live? | In river valleys, coastal plains and fertile lowlands | In deserts and high mountains, where land is cheap and empty | Spread evenly over all the land | Mostly in the cold polar lands
exercise: Since about when have more than half of all people lived in towns and cities? | Since about 2007 | Since about 1337 | Since about 1800 | That has never happened
exercise: What is a megacity? | A city of more than 10 million people | Any city with a cathedral | A city of more than 10,000 people | A capital city of a big country
exercise: Which of these is a megacity? | Lagos | Oxford | Reykjavik | Canberra
exercise: How big were the largest cities of the 1300s? | A few hundred thousand people at most | Well over 10 million people each, like today | About 1,000 people | About the size of Tokyo today
exercise: What share of Europe's people did the Black Death kill? | Perhaps a third to a half | About one in a hundred | Almost everybody | Only animals died
exercise: When did the Black Death sweep through Europe? | Between 1347 and 1351 | Between 1914 and 1918 | Between 1066 and 1070 | Between 1600 and 1700
exercise: What does population density mean? | How many people live on each square kilometre | How many people live in a whole country | How tall the buildings in a city are | How old most people in a country are
exercise: Which country has over 1,000 people per square kilometre? | Bangladesh | Australia | Canada | Kazakhstan
exercise: Which country has a very young population? | Niger | Japan | Italy | Germany
exercise: Where did most great cities grow? | At river crossings, harbours and trade-route meeting points | On the tops of the highest mountains, where they were safe from attack | In the middle of deserts far from water | Wherever a king happened to be born
exercise: What is migration? | People moving to live in another place | Counting the people in a town | Birds flying south in winter only | Cities growing taller buildings
test: My mate says the world has always had about 8 billion people. Right? | No, around 1337 there were perhaps only about 400 million | Yes, the number of people on Earth has hardly changed in a thousand years | No, there used to be many more than now | Yes, but they lived only in Europe
test: Why do so few people live in the Sahara? | There is too little water to farm | The sand is too hot to build on at all | It is against the law to live there | It is too cold at night for anyone
test: Egypt's people are packed in a thin strip. Where is it? | Along the River Nile | Along the Red Sea mountains | In the middle of the Sahara | Around the oases of Libya
test: Why did London grow where it did? | It was the lowest point where the Thames could be bridged | It was on the highest hill in England, safe from floods and enemies | It was the farthest place from the sea | A king picked a random spot on a map
test: In the game, a small rich province can hold more people than a huge empty one. Is that true of real countries? | Yes, Bangladesh has far more people than huge Australia | No, bigger countries always have more people | No, population depends only on the number of cities | Yes, but only in the 1300s
test: Japan has lots of old people and few babies. Why might it worry? | Fewer young workers to look after everyone later | Old people use up all the land, so no new homes can ever be built | Babies are not allowed in Japan | The country will get bigger in size
test: Niger's median age is under 16. What does that mean? | Half its people are younger than 16 | Nobody there is older than 16 | Children there start work at 16 | Its people live only to 16
test: Gran: "Most people live in villages, always have." Is that true today? | No, since about 2007 most people live in towns and cities | Yes, villages still hold most people in the world today, as they always did | Yes, and cities are getting smaller | No, most people now live in megacities alone
test: Which continent has the most megacities? | Asia | Antarctica | Europe | Australia
test: My uncle moved from Kerala to Dubai for a job. What is that called? | Migration | Population density | Urban sprawl | Colonisation
test: Why can a small country hold more people than a huge one? | Its land may be flatter, wetter and better for farming | Small countries make people live longer, so more of them are alive at once | Big countries do not allow cities | People are counted twice in small countries
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the majority of the world's great cities arose at particular locations. | Such locations afforded river crossings, sheltered harbours or the convergence of trade routes | Such locations were selected at random by the rulers of the period, without regard to geography | Such locations were invariably elevated mountain summits | Such locations were distant from all sources of water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises population density? | The number of inhabitants per square kilometre of territory | The aggregate number of inhabitants of a sovereign state | The proportion of inhabitants residing in towns and cities | The average age of the inhabitants of a territory
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the most populous states in 2026. | India and China each comprise approximately 1.4 billion persons | The United States comprises the greatest number of persons of any state | Russia comprises more persons than India and China combined | China has never been exceeded in population by any state
exam: It is given that the Black Death traversed Europe between 1347 and 1351. Ascertain the proportion of the population of Europe it is estimated to have destroyed. | Perhaps one third to one half | Approximately one per cent | Approximately ninety-nine per cent | None whatsoever, it affected livestock alone
